# Sentry BUILD-TIME credentials. Copy to `.env.sentry-build-plugin` and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.sentry-build-plugin.example .env.sentry-build-plugin
#
# The real file is gitignored (see .gitignore). Unlike the DSN in `.env` — which
# is a write-only client key that ships inside the IPA anyway — the auth token
# below is a GENUINE SECRET. It can read and write your Sentry org. Never commit
# it, never paste it into app.json, and never give it more scope than listed.
#
# Why this filename: `sentry-xcode.sh` and `sentry-xcode-debug-files.sh` look for
# `.env.sentry-build-plugin` at the React Native project root and export it as
# SENTRY_DOTENV_PATH automatically. That matters because Xcode build phases do
# NOT inherit your interactive shell environment — exporting SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN in
# .zshrc and then hitting Archive in Xcode.app will NOT work.

# Generate at: Sentry → Settings → Auth Tokens → Create New Token.
# Required scopes: project:releases  (and org:read for release association).
# Nothing broader is needed for source-map + dSYM upload.
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=

# --- Escape hatches (leave unset for a real release build) -------------------
# Without a valid token the upload step emits `error:` and FAILS the Xcode
# Archive. That is the correct default — a release that silently skipped its
# source maps produces unreadable stack traces and nobody notices until the
# first crash. Set one of these only when you deliberately want a build without
# symbolication:
#
#   SENTRY_ALLOW_FAILURE=true        # attempt upload; downgrade failure to a warning
#   SENTRY_DISABLE_AUTO_UPLOAD=true  # skip the upload entirely
